// A failed match which got somewhere before it failed. Scanning "-" enters the state which only ARROW can complete,
// then runs out of transitions on "a" without ever having passed an accepting state. The failed match is the bytes the
// automaton consumed; the byte it merely looked at is not one of them, and consuming that one as well throws away the
// start of the token which follows.
//
// The case asserts on the token after the error and not on the length of the error itself, because a trace records
// where an error started and never how far it ran. A scanner with the defect reports IDENT "b" here, which is an
// ordinary successful match with nothing about it to say that a byte of it was eaten - so the token which follows the
// error is the only place the defect is visible at all.
//
// The input ends on a second "-" so that a failed match which runs into the end of the input is covered as well.
//
// The parser is not what this case is about, so the grammar is the single empty production the file format requires.

@scanner {
    ARROW: "->";

    IDENT: /[a-z]+/;
}

@parser {
    tokens
        : @empty
        ;
}
